  "body": "Wanted to ask about https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#quotes which lists rules in the format\r\n```\r\n:root:lang(af), :not(:lang(af)) > :lang(af)\r\n```\r\nrather than\r\n```\r\n:root:lang(af), [lang]:lang(af)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe latter pattern will trigger the rule on elements that are restating a language, which is the only different effect from the rule in the HTML spec. That has no actual effect on the element unless the author set something different on one of its ancestors.\r\n\r\nCurrently the CSS specs that require language-specific rules use the latter format, HTML uses the former. I was wondering, which format should we all use and why? I imagine the most performant rule would be our preference, given the difference between the two in terms of effect is mostly unnoticeable.\r\n\r\nCC @emilio ",
